Subject: RegExp: required-literal prefilter and anchored entry from static analysis of the bytecode

What this does
  - libregexp.c: `lre_analyze` walks the compiled instruction stream of a non-sticky pattern. Jumps are treated as covering the instructions they pass over; the longest run of exact-character instructions that no jump skips (save instructions are transparent) is the required literal. A forward pass over the prefix gives the most code units a match can start before the literal (unbounded if a loop, back reference or lookbehind precedes it). A pattern whose first instruction after the match start is a non-multiline `^` is marked anchored. Nothing is added to the serialized bytecode.
  - quickjs.c: the analysis is cached per context by bytecode string (512 slots, the slot holds a reference). The generic matcher path uses it: an anchored pattern runs from index 0 through `lre_exec_at` and fails at once for any other start index; a pattern with a required literal returns "no match" when the subject has no occurrence at or after the start index (memchr + compare for 8-bit strings), and starts scanning at the first occurrence minus the prefix bound. `JS_REGEXP_CAPTURE_STACK_SIZE` rises from 16 to 64 so patterns with up to about 30 groups need no heap capture buffer.

Why we need it
  Census: one pattern with a required literal after a group was 42.6% of matcher cycles (26,520 scans of 2,264 characters, no hit); `/^ba/` ran 1.16M scans of 5 characters; an 11-group URL pattern allocated a capture buffer per exec.

Soundness
  Every match contains the literal at or after its start and the start is at most prefix_max units before it, so skipping to that point or failing cannot lose a match; `^` without the multiline flag matches only at index 0. Case-insensitive classes use other instructions and are never part of the literal. Literals with units above 0xff cannot occur in an 8-bit subject. Lookahead interiors and loop bodies are covered by their jump and excluded.

Failure modes
  Patterns with bytecode over 4096 bytes are not analysed. The cache holds up to 512 bytecode strings per context until the context is freed.

